course: dictionary
lesson: 1013
title: Bonfire words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bonfire is a large fire, lit outdoors, to burn rubbish or to celebrate something. You may not know the word yet, but the words around it give clues. «Big stars live fast and die young, like a huge bonfire that burns out quickly.» The words huge, outdoors and burns out quickly help you picture it.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. Does «a huge large outdoor fire that burns out quickly» still make sense? If yes, you are on the right track.
explain: Bonfires are linked to celebrations. In the UK, Bonfire Night in November has bonfires and fireworks. In Ireland, people light bonfires on Halloween or midsummer. Knowing where a word is used helps you understand it.
fact: A bonfire is a large outdoor fire, made to burn rubbish or for a celebration.
fact: Bonfire Night, on 5 November, is a celebration in the UK and New Zealand with bonfires and fireworks.
fact: Safety rules: keep well back, never throw aerosol cans into a bonfire, and an adult must be in charge.
fact: If clothes catch fire, remember stop, drop and roll.
fact: Burning is a chemical change that needs fuel, heat and oxygen.
exercise: «Big stars live fast and die young, like a huge bonfire that burns out quickly.» What is a bonfire? | A small candle in a jar | *A large outdoor fire | A pile of cold stones | A firework that sings
exercise: Which is a safe bonfire rule? | Stand as close as you can to feel the heat | Throw spray cans into the fire to make it bigger | Run around the fire to keep warm | *Keep well back and let an adult be in charge
exercise: In science, what does burning need to keep going? | *Fuel, heat and oxygen | Music and light | Snow and ice | Water and sand
test: What should you do if your clothes catch fire? | Shout and wave your arms | Run as fast as you can | Jump into a big pile of leaves | *Stop, drop and roll
test: Why is a bonfire pile checked before it is lit? | Because bonfires must be lit by dogs | To count the leaves | *Because a hedgehog may be curled up inside it | To make the fire hotter
exam: «Hedgehogs are getting rarer in Britain, so small things matter: a bonfire pile checked before it is lit.» Why does this help hedgehogs? | Hedgehogs like the smell of smoke | Hedgehogs need the fire to keep warm | It makes the bonfire burn faster | *It stops a hedgehog being hurt while it sleeps in the pile
